--- CRITERION 5: render test asserts rate+money in headline AND table body,
    asserts the new header labels, asserts colspan matches column count in both
    modes -- FAIL
The two tests pass (`go test ./internal/handlers/whatif/ -run TestWhatIfTaxOptimizer
-v` -> both --- PASS), and the fixture does use distinctive values
(PeakMarginalBracket 40.5 -> "40", TotalRothConverted 123456 -> "$123,456.00").
But passing is not the question; whether the assertions bind is. I mutated the
template one change at a time, ran the tests, then restored from a byte-exact backup
and re-verified with `sha256sum -c` after every probe.

  MUTATION A -- deleted both new body <td> cells (template lines 99-100), leaving
    only the headline references (verified by grep: only lines 28-29 still mention
    PeakMarginalBracket/TotalRothConverted).
    RESULT: `ok  budget2/internal/handlers/whatif  0.046s` -- TESTS PASSED.
    => The block commented "// Table body: Best candidate's formatted peak rate and
       converted amount" does NOT assert table-body rendering. Its probes
       strings.Contains(flat,"40%") and strings.Contains(out,"$123,456.00") are
       already satisfied by the headline, which is in the same `out` string.
    => The "$0.00" probe (test lines 94-96, commented "Zero-value candidate must
       render $0.00, not blank") is likewise not bound to the Roth Conv column: it
       still passed with that column deleted, because the Delta-vs-Baseline cell
       renders $0.00 for the candidate whose ending portfolio equals the baseline.

  MUTATION B -- deleted both new <th> headers (template lines 65-70), leaving only
    the glossary <dt>s (verified by grep: "Peak Rate"/"Roth Conv" remain only at
    lines 160 and 162).
    RESULT: `ok  budget2/internal/handlers/whatif  0.038s` -- TESTS PASSED.
    => The loop at test lines 79-83 ("New header labels") does NOT assert the header
       labels. Bare strings.Contains(out,"Peak Rate") is satisfied by the glossary
       entry the same test separately asserts at lines 99-106.

  MUTATION C -- reverted colspan to the stale 7/5.
    RESULT: `--- FAIL: TestWhatIfTaxOptimizer_ColspanMatchesColumnCount` -- caught.

  MUTATION D -- deleted the two headline lines (28-29).
    RESULT: `--- FAIL: TestWhatIfTaxOptimizer_RendersPeakRateAndRothConverted`
      tax_optimizer_render_test.go:72: expected headline peak marginal rate
      tax_optimizer_render_test.go:75: expected headline Roth converted amount
    -- caught.

WHAT IS MISSING, precisely (internal/handlers/whatif/tax_optimizer_render_test.go):
 (a) Table-body assertion. Lines 85-91 must scope the search to the <tbody> (or to a
     single data row) rather than to the whole rendered document, so that deleting
     the <td> cells fails the test.
 (b) Header-label assertion. Lines 79-83 must scope to the <thead> / assert the
     <th> markup, so that deleting the <th> cells fails the test. As written it
     cannot distinguish a header from the glossary.
 (c) TestWhatIfTaxOptimizer_ColspanMatchesColumnCount does not match colspan against
     a column count -- it compares against the hard-coded literals 7 and 9. Both
     mutation A and mutation B break the real header/body column agreement (A leaves
     7 headers vs 5 body cells in non-MC; B leaves 5 headers vs 7 body cells) and
     BOTH passed. To assert what its name claims the test must count the rendered
     <th> in <thead> and compare that number to the colspan attribute, in each mode.
 (d) Suggested, given (a): the zero-value probe at lines 94-96 should assert the
     Roth Conv cell specifically, since $0.00 also arises from the delta column.

